            <ArticleTitle>Effect of obesity on patient-reported outcomes in sarcoidosis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>559-64</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.5588/ijtld.12.0665</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To assess the impact of high body mass index (BMI) on patient-reported outcomes in sarcoidosis patients and healthy persons.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">In this case-control study, we investigated symptoms of fatigue and dyspnoea, health status, BMI and spirometric tests in 184 sarcoidosis patients and the same number of sex- and age-matched healthy subjects. Fatigue was assessed using the fatigue scale (FS), dyspnoea was determined by the baseline dyspnoea index (BDI) and health status was measured using the respiratory-specific St George's Respiratory Questionnaire (SGRQ).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">There were significantly more subjects with increased BMI (≥25 kg/m(2)) among the sarcoidosis patients than among the healthy volunteers ((2) 37.675, P &lt; 0.01). Sarcoidosis patients also had a greater probability of having a higher BMI (P &lt; 0.01, OR 1.18, 95%CI 1.071.3). We found significantly lower BDI scores and forced expiratory volume in 1 s/forced vital capacity, as well as higher total SGRQ and total FS scores in sarcoidosis patients than in healthy individuals (P &lt; 0.01 for all differences).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Sarcoidosis significantly reduces patients' health status, both independently and also due to increased BMI. Reduction in BMI may contribute to improved spirometry results and health status of patients with sarcoidosis.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
